Title: "Boolean Set Operations"
Id: "455f0c6a-e9f8-48c7-af05-4885209a1483"


Text: "From basic sets, we construct new sets through systematic Boolean
       operations analogous to propositional connectives. The most fundamental
       operations are union, corresponding to logical disjunction, and
       intersection, corresponding to logical conjunction."
Id: "22fc007f-953e-4b7d-9b81-5955ecef969a"


[A \.cup./ B]
Defines: C is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "set union $A \cup B$"
. written: "A? \cup B?"
. description: "The set containing all elements belonging to set A, set B, or
                both."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{14}
Id: "aa8fc235-5ae6-43ce-9640-514787dbc8d2"


[A \.cap./ B]
Defines: C is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "set intersection $A \cap B$"
. written: "A? \cap B?"
. description: "The set containing all elements belonging simultaneously to both
                A and B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{15}
Id: "cad89972-0b51-46b5-b6d4-f591760b2871"


Text: "Set difference, or relative complement, subtracts the elements of one set
       from another. Symmetric difference captures elements that belong to
       exactly one of two sets, corresponding to the Boolean exclusive-or (XOR)."
Id: "5e84704d-1692-4427-b36d-f2a1555bff14"


[A \.setminus./ B]
Defines: C is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "set difference $A \setminus B$"
. written: "A? \setminus B?"
. description: "The relative complement of B in A: the set of all elements in A
                that do not belong to B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{18}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{17}
Id: "93ab3dc0-f227-4368-a9a8-fd1978906b99"


[A \.symm.diff./ B]
Defines: C is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "symmetric difference $A \mathbin{\Delta} B$"
. written: "A? \mathbin{\Delta} B?"
. description: "The set of elements belonging to either A or B but not both: (A
                \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A)."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{18}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{18}
Id: "eb83af5b-db23-4696-8e6f-c9ef1c437885"


Text: "When all sets under investigation are subsets of a designated universal
       set U, the complement of subset A relative to U is called the absolute
       complement of A, denoted A^c = U \setminus A."
Id: "436a7c7b-31d4-4487-b429-1d8116ca0473"


[\absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U}]
Defines: Ac is \set
when:
. A is \set
. U is \set
Documented:
. called: "absolute complement $A^c$ in universal set $U$"
. written: "A?^c"
. description: "The complement of subset A with respect to ambient universal set
                U: U \setminus A."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{19}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "63751815-dca6-4f7e-b9c3-e344bcca83a6"


Text: "The duality between union and intersection is captured by De Morgan's
       Laws for sets: the complement of a union is the intersection of the
       complements, and the complement of an intersection is the union of the
       complements."
Id: "0d8573f7-0135-4b3f-81ae-33c7208397c4"


[\de.morgan.set.union]
Theorem:
given: A, B, U is \set
then:
. (\absolute.complement:of{(A \.cup./ B)}:in{U} = (\absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U} \.cap./ \absolute.complement:of{B}:in{U}))
Documented:
. called: "De Morgan's First Law for Sets"
. written: "(A \cup B)^c = A^c \cap B^c"
. description: "The complement of a union is the intersection of the individual
                complements."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{20}
Id: "fb474876-26a9-4dc7-81ae-cc120b2ae5ef"


[\de.morgan.set.intersection]
Theorem:
given: A, B, U is \set
then:
. (\absolute.complement:of{(A \.cap./ B)}:in{U} = (\absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U} \.cup./ \absolute.complement:of{B}:in{U}))
Documented:
. called: "De Morgan's Second Law for Sets"
. written: "(A \cap B)^c = A^c \cup B^c"
. description: "The complement of an intersection is the union of the individual
                complements."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{20}
Id: "84519427-0855-4f12-9c11-b2e9c78386b7"


Text: "Set operations satisfy algebraic identities directly mirroring those of
       propositional logic. In particular, intersection distributes over union,
       and union distributes over intersection."
Id: "a696048c-3a26-45a0-bacb-7e6ed8519935"


[\distributive.law.sets]
Theorem:
given: A, B, C is \set
then:
. ((A \.cap./ (B \.cup./ C)) = ((A \.cap./ B) \.cup./ (A \.cap./ C)))
Documented:
. called: "distributivity of intersection over union"
. written: "A \cap (B \cup C) = (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)"
. description: "The algebraic distributive law of set intersection over set
                union."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{20}
Id: "32516ef7-bbcc-4244-8e05-b0f4ffb6730c"


Text: "Under these operations, the power set \mathcal{P}(U) forms a Boolean
       algebra (\mathcal{P}(U), \cup, \cap, {}^c, \emptyset, U). The empty set
       acts as zero (bottom), U acts as unit (top), and each subset A satisfies
       the complementation laws."
Id: "17d25f33-de0a-4f0e-93ad-f283a31a0a8d"


Theorem:
given: A, U is \set
where:
. A \.subseteq./ U
then:
. ((A \.cup./ \absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U}) = U)
. ((A \.cap./ \absolute.complement:of{A}:in{U}) = \empty.set)
Documented:
. called: "complementation identities of the Boolean algebra of sets"
. written: "A \cup A^c = U \land A \cap A^c = \emptyset"
. description: "The fundamental lattice complementation laws guaranteeing that a
                subset and its complement partition the universal set."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{21}
Id: "a275e13b-840d-4f75-8db1-94577eee586f"
